<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Viscounty of Narbonne</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1507 CE (approx.) · capital Narbonne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The viscounty of the old Roman port of Narbonne, famed under Viscountess Ermengard as a centre of troubadour culture and Mediterranean trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Viscounts appear at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> around 900 as deputies of the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">Toulouse</a>, who had inherited authority over the former Carolingian march of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a>. The office became hereditary, and the viscounts shared power in the city with its powerful archbishops.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> was a busy port and a centre of Jewish learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermengarde-of-narbonne">Viscountess Ermengard</a>, who ruled from 1134 to 1192, presided over a court celebrated by troubadours and steered between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. Later viscounts survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> by siding with the crusaders, while the river's shift and the silting of the port slowly cut the city's trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The viscounty passed to the house of Foix in the 15th century. In 1507 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-de-foix-duke-of-nemours">Gaston de Foix</a> exchanged it with his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII</a> for the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-nemours">Nemours</a>, and it was joined to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 900 CE – 1507 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.61%;width:12.08%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1507 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Narbonne</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ermengard of Narbonne, Gaston de Foix, Duke of Nemours</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Narbonne</dd></dl></aside></div>
